# DKIM2 delayed-bounce demo address.
#
# reflector-delayedbounce@dkim2.com is accepted at RCPT (250), then deliberately
# fails at DELIVERY, so that Postfix's own bounce(8) originates a real DSN. That
# DSN is then DKIM2-signed by the outbound milter (see main.cf:
# internal_mail_filter_classes = bounce). This demonstrates draft §11/§12 with a
# genuinely Postfix-generated bounce — NOT the hand-built reflector-dsn path.
#
# It routes to the "dkim2-delayedbounce" pipe(8) transport (see
# postfix-dkim2-reflect.master.cf), whose delivery agent always exits with a
# permanent failure. It deliberately does NOT use the "error:" transport:
# error: rejects the recipient at RCPT time for SMTP clients (a synchronous
# 550), which is not a *delayed* bounce. Accepting at RCPT and failing at
# delivery is what produces the asynchronous, MTA-originated DSN.
#
# This map is referenced by BOTH:
#   transport_maps       - route the recipient to the failing pipe transport
#                          so a permanent delivery failure (and thus a
#                          Postfix-generated bounce) results.
#   local_recipient_maps - so the address is accepted at RCPT TO (the value's
#                          key is all that matters for acceptance).
#
# Install:
#   cp deploy/postfix-dkim2-delayedbounce /etc/postfix/dkim2-delayedbounce
#   postmap /etc/postfix/dkim2-delayedbounce
# Append "hash:/etc/postfix/dkim2-delayedbounce" to BOTH transport_maps and
# local_recipient_maps in main.cf; add the dkim2-delayedbounce pipe service to
# master.cf (see postfix-dkim2-reflect.master.cf); then: postfix reload
reflector-delayedbounce@dkim2.com  dkim2-delayedbounce:
